	<Placemark>
		<name>Red — Highest flood extent</name>
		<styleUrl>#label-red</styleUrl>
		<description><![CDATA[Highest flood extent, 2016-2026. Outermost boundary.

Represents 25 July 2024, the maximum stage on this reach in the decade. Bund Garden reached 543.40 m at 88,888 cusecs in the evening, about 0.95 m above the 542.45 m warning line, after a morning reading of 542.60 m at 69,111 cusecs. Mundhwa bridge, Holkar bridge and other low crossings were under water. Hundreds of families were moved from Shantinagar, Bharatnagar, Qatarwadi, Indranagar and Yerawada. A later pulse on 4 August 2024 raised Khadakwasla discharge to about 45,705 cusecs but did not exceed the 25 July Bund Garden stage.

Offset from the traced bank: about 79 m at the Sangam end to about 167 m at the Kharadi end (lower-flood offset plus 1.76 m of extra stage).

Indicative historical flood boundaries for the Mula-Mutha corridor drawn in this file, for 2016-2026. They are offsets from the traced river bank, scaled with published river levels. They are not a surveyed flood line, not a HEC-RAS inundation map, and not a classification of archived satellite pixels.

Ranking uses Water Resources Department levels at Bund Garden barrage, as released under RTI and reported on 2 September 2025: the warning (blue) line is 542.45 m; 25 July 2024 reached 543.40 m at 88,888 cusecs (highest stage in this decade); 20 August 2025 reached 542.70 m at 71,408 cusecs. The lower line represents the 4 August 2016 Khadakwasla release of about 39,000 cusecs and the 5 August 2019 release of 45,474 cusecs. That 2019 discharge plots near 541.64 m on the 24-25 July 2024 Bund Garden rating segment (69,111 cusecs at 542.60 m to 88,888 cusecs at 543.40 m), below the warning line. Khadakwasla releases in 2026 peaked near 27,203 cusecs (9 July) and 29,177 cusecs (4 August), inside this lower envelope.

Yellow sits 35 m outside the traced bank at the confined Sangam end and 70 m outside it at the Mundhwa-Kharadi end. Orange and red step further out by the extra stage above 541.64 m (1.06 m and 1.76 m) times a side slope of 25 m horizontal per metre of stage upstream, opening to 55 m per metre downstream. Upstream of Sangam (Khadakwasla to the confluence, including Sinhagad Road) is outside this KML.]]></description>
		<Point><coordinates>73.9639533,18.5468094,0</coordinates></Point>
	</Placemark>

	<Placemark>
		<name>Orange — Medium flood extent</name>
		<styleUrl>#label-orange</styleUrl>
		<description><![CDATA[Medium flood extent, 2016-2026.

Represents 20 August 2025, when the Mula-Mutha at Bund Garden reached 542.70 m on a flow of 71,408 cusecs, about 0.25 m above the 542.45 m warning line. That week Khadakwasla itself was releasing about 35,000-39,190 cusecs; Mula inflows made the combined flow at Bund Garden higher. PMC evacuated low-lying pockets including Yerawada, Mangalwar Peth, Bhimnagar, Patil Estate and Warje, and closed Baba Bhide, Tilak and Kharadi bridges.

Offset from the traced bank: about 62 m at the Sangam end to about 128 m at the Kharadi end (lower-flood offset plus 1.06 m of extra stage).

Indicative historical flood boundaries for the Mula-Mutha corridor drawn in this file, for 2016-2026. They are offsets from the traced river bank, scaled with published river levels. They are not a surveyed flood line, not a HEC-RAS inundation map, and not a classification of archived satellite pixels.

Ranking uses Water Resources Department levels at Bund Garden barrage, as released under RTI and reported on 2 September 2025: the warning (blue) line is 542.45 m; 25 July 2024 reached 543.40 m at 88,888 cusecs (highest stage in this decade); 20 August 2025 reached 542.70 m at 71,408 cusecs. The lower line represents the 4 August 2016 Khadakwasla release of about 39,000 cusecs and the 5 August 2019 release of 45,474 cusecs. That 2019 discharge plots near 541.64 m on the 24-25 July 2024 Bund Garden rating segment (69,111 cusecs at 542.60 m to 88,888 cusecs at 543.40 m), below the warning line. Khadakwasla releases in 2026 peaked near 27,203 cusecs (9 July) and 29,177 cusecs (4 August), inside this lower envelope.

Yellow sits 35 m outside the traced bank at the confined Sangam end and 70 m outside it at the Mundhwa-Kharadi end. Orange and red step further out by the extra stage above 541.64 m (1.06 m and 1.76 m) times a side slope of 25 m horizontal per metre of stage upstream, opening to 55 m per metre downstream. Upstream of Sangam (Khadakwasla to the confluence, including Sinhagad Road) is outside this KML.]]></description>
		<Point><coordinates>73.9246871,18.5404267,0</coordinates></Point>
	</Placemark>

	<Placemark>
		<name>Yellow — Lower flood extent</name>
		<styleUrl>#label-yellow</styleUrl>
		<description><![CDATA[Lower flood extent, 2016-2026.

Represents the August 2016 and August 2019 class of flood, the lower of the three historical envelopes. On 4 August 2016 Khadakwasla discharged about 39,000 cusecs and submerged Baba Bhide bridge; low-lying houses off Sinhagad Road were evacuated. On 5 August 2019 the dam discharged 45,474 cusecs, the highest Khadakwasla release since 2011 but still below the 2024 and 2025 Bund Garden stages. 2026 releases (about 27,000-29,000 cusecs) stayed inside this line.

Offset from the traced bank: 35 m at the western (Sangam) end to 70 m at the eastern (Mundhwa-Kharadi) end.

Indicative historical flood boundaries for the Mula-Mutha corridor drawn in this file, for 2016-2026. They are offsets from the traced river bank, scaled with published river levels. They are not a surveyed flood line, not a HEC-RAS inundation map, and not a classification of archived satellite pixels.

Ranking uses Water Resources Department levels at Bund Garden barrage, as released under RTI and reported on 2 September 2025: the warning (blue) line is 542.45 m; 25 July 2024 reached 543.40 m at 88,888 cusecs (highest stage in this decade); 20 August 2025 reached 542.70 m at 71,408 cusecs. The lower line represents the 4 August 2016 Khadakwasla release of about 39,000 cusecs and the 5 August 2019 release of 45,474 cusecs. That 2019 discharge plots near 541.64 m on the 24-25 July 2024 Bund Garden rating segment (69,111 cusecs at 542.60 m to 88,888 cusecs at 543.40 m), below the warning line. Khadakwasla releases in 2026 peaked near 27,203 cusecs (9 July) and 29,177 cusecs (4 August), inside this lower envelope.

Yellow sits 35 m outside the traced bank at the confined Sangam end and 70 m outside it at the Mundhwa-Kharadi end. Orange and red step further out by the extra stage above 541.64 m (1.06 m and 1.76 m) times a side slope of 25 m horizontal per metre of stage upstream, opening to 55 m per metre downstream. Upstream of Sangam (Khadakwasla to the confluence, including Sinhagad Road) is outside this KML.]]></description>
		<Point><coordinates>73.8844910,18.5448958,0</coordinates></Point>
	</Placemark>

